.dart_tool/
.packages
.pub/
build/
.flutter-plugins
.flutter-plugins-dependencies
.idea/
.vscode/
*.iml
android/.gradle/
ios/Pods/
ios/.symlinks/

# Machine-specific paths written by `flutter pub get` / the platform builds.
# These embed the absolute SDK + project path of whoever ran the tool last,
# so tracking them makes every Linux<->Windows switch a spurious diff.
# All of them are regenerated automatically — never commit them.
android/local.properties
ios/Flutter/Generated.xcconfig
ios/Flutter/flutter_export_environment.sh
ios/Flutter/ephemeral/

# Release signing. `key.properties` holds the keystore passwords in plaintext
# and the keystore itself is the one artifact that cannot be regenerated — lose
# it and no future build can ever update an app already on Play. Keep both out
# of the repo (see `android/key.properties.example`); the patterns are broad on
# purpose, so a keystore dropped anywhere in the tree is still ignored.
android/key.properties
*.jks
*.p12
*.keystore

# Superpowers brainstorm tooling state
.superpowers/

# The queue's own routing destination. `CaptureRouter` appends whatever you
# press `R` on to this file, so it fills with unfiltered thought — the raw
# capture, not a summary of it. Tracking it publishes every future note the
# moment someone runs `git add -A`, which is a channel no secret scan would
# ever flag, because nothing has leaked yet. The file still works exactly as
# before: it stays on disk, and the coding agent reading this repository finds
# it there. Delivery was always the point; version control never was.
inbox.md

# Worktrees created by Claude Code sessions — each is its own git repo.
.claude/worktrees/

# Per-machine tool permissions. Not secret, but it is local state that nobody
# else can use, and it is the one file under .claude/ that a blanket `git add`
# would otherwise sweep in. Anything genuinely shared (project agents, skills)
# stays trackable — this deliberately names one file rather than the directory.
.claude/settings.local.json

# A byte-for-byte copy of CLAUDE.md kept for another agent CLI. Two files
# holding the same guidance drift apart the first time one is edited, so the
# copy stays local rather than becoming a second source of truth in the repo.
gemini.md

# Hand-off briefs written by the app itself. These are the user's own captured
# thoughts — meeting notes, client work, half-formed ideas — landing in whatever
# repo the capture was filed under. They are input to an agent, never source.
.agent-tasks/

# Local macOS code-signing identity (see LocalSigning.xcconfig.example).
# Untracked like android/key.properties: naming a machine's own certificate,
# and absent by default so a clone still builds ad-hoc.
macos/Runner/Configs/LocalSigning.xcconfig

# Adversarial test runs. Each report names the exact carrier that got past a
# control — the forged fence marker, the CLI flag a capture body can smuggle —
# and this repository is public, so a report is a working recipe against every
# install that has not taken the fix yet. The *fixes* and their regression
# cases are tracked (`test/adversarial_injection_test.dart`); the write-up that
# explains how to reproduce them is not. Ignored as a directory, because the
# next run's file name is not known here.
docs/security/

# Wrangler's local state. `cache/wrangler-account.json` was tracked here and
# held only an account id and an email — but `wrangler login` writes an
# `oauth_token` and a `refresh_token` into this same directory, and the
# pre-commit hook only refuses `.agent-tasks/` and `inbox.md`, so nothing
# would have stopped the next login from being committed. Ignored as a
# directory rather than by file, because which files Wrangler puts here is
# its decision and not ours.
.wrangler/
